@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: what --credentials broker|forward actually does on the sbx backend, and who injects
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go
' covers: internal/sbx/sbx.go
'
' ── THE AXIS IS REAL HERE, UNLIKE THE TIER ─────────────────────────────────
' The egress tier is inert on sbx (_spec/internal/sbx/policy-baseline.puml).
' --credentials is NOT. Measured with `--print` and dummy values:
'
'   broker    argv carries NOTHING for the credential names. proveo pushes them
'             into sbx's store host-side (`sbx secret set --force`, value on
'             stdin) and sbx's own proxy injects them as headers at egress and
'             materialises ~/.claude/.credentials.json. The agent process never
'             holds the value.
'   forward   argv carries a BARE `-e NAME`. sbx passes the host's value straight
'             into the container and the agent reads it directly.
'
' In NEITHER mode does a credential VALUE appear in the argv or in the Kit.
'
' ── THE NAME MEANS A DIFFERENT PARTY THAN ON DOCKER ────────────────────────
' On docker, "broker" means PROVEO's mitmproxy holds the key and injects at the
' proxy. On sbx there is no proveo egress layer at all: "broker" means proveo
' steps aside and SBX brokers. The guarantee — the value never reaches the agent
' — is the same; the party is not. The label is kept because sbx genuinely is
' acting as the broker there, so the word stays true even though the mechanism
' behind it changes with the backend.
'
' ── WHY PROVEO DECLARES NO CREDENTIAL IN THE KIT ───────────────────────────
' sbx.Kit has no Credentials field, deliberately. proveo's Kit is a MIXIN, and a
' mixin that repeats a service the built-in agent already declares is rejected
' outright ("defined in both"). Injection policy is sbx's to own.
'
' sandbox.KitCredentials used to build exactly that policy — one entry per
' service, proxyManaged, an inject rule per host — and it was DEAD: nothing in
' production called it, only three tests, which is precisely why it read as live.
' Deleted, along with the sbx.KitCredential/KitAPIKey/KitInject types that existed
' only to be its return shape. Nothing is left that could be mistaken for proveo
' declaring an injection policy it does not own.
'
' ── THE STORE IS HOST-WIDE, AND THAT IS THE HAZARD ─────────────────────────
' `sbx secret set --force` writes to a store shared by EVERY sandbox on the host,
' and the entry outlives the run. A stale entry from an earlier run can therefore
' authenticate a later one that never chose it — which reads as proveo picking a
' credential the operator did not, somewhere they cannot see. A run that writes
' says so once, naming `sbx secret ls`.
'
' Same shape as the policy baseline: a per-run choice with a host-global effect.
' The difference is that proveo DOES write here, because it must — the credential
' cannot reach sbx's proxy any other way — whereas it only ever reports the
' baseline.
'
' ── THE WORKSPACE .env IS READ INSIDE THE SANDBOX (measured) ───────────────
' Separate from broker/forward, and easy to miss: on the DOCKER backend proveo
' masks every .env* with /dev/null on the enforced tiers. sbx does no such thing.
' It takes directory workspaces, the repo carrying those files is mounted whole,
' and the Kit sets no PROVEO_EGRESS_MODE — so in-container ShouldSkipEnvLoad("")
' is FALSE and the prelude sources the .env with `set -a`. Every key in it becomes
' agent environment, whatever egress tier was chosen.
'
' proveo now says so: WarnMountedSecrets takes a `sandboxed` flag rather than
' checking the tier, because the tier that legitimately silences docker was
' silencing the backend that actually reads the file. Whether sbx SHOULD load a
' workspace .env is a capability question and is not settled here.
'
' ── D1, RESOLVED ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' proveo used to emit `-e VAR=` (empty) on the theory that sbx's global store
' would otherwise inject a stale value in front of a mounted login. Probed against
' sbx v0.39.0 with both keys in the store and no -e flag: both arrive UNSET. sbx
' does not export service secrets as environment variables. The empty value was
' not merely useless — an agent reads a SET variable as a chosen credential
' whatever its value, so a blank one occupied the slot the login needed. Removed.
title --credentials on sbx — who holds the key, and who injects it

participant "operator" as OP <<app>>
participant "proveo" as PV <<app>>
participant "sbx secret store\nHOST-WIDE" as STORE <<data>>
participant "sbx proxy" as PROXY <<cloud>>
participant "agent\nin the sandbox" as AG <<app>>

OP ARROW_MAIN PV : --credentials broker (default)
PV ARROW_MAIN STORE : sbx secret set --force NAME (stdin)
STORE ARROW_QUEUE PROXY : proxy-managed
PROXY ARROW_MAIN AG : header injected at egress;\n.credentials.json materialised
PV ARROW_OPTIONAL AG : argv carries NO name and NO value

OP ARROW_MAIN PV : --credentials forward
PV ARROW_ERROR AG : bare `-e NAME` — the host value\nlands in the agent's environment

note over STORE
  **Shared, and it persists.** Every sandbox on
  the machine sees this store, and ""--force""
  overwrites. What one run writes can decide a
  later run's identity. A run that writes says
  so once and names ""sbx secret ls"".
end note

note over PV
  **proveo declares no injection policy.** The Kit
  is a mixin; sbx rejects one that repeats a
  service its built-in agent owns. The code that
  built such a policy was dead and is gone.
end note
@enduml
